   <h3> Raids and Preparation</h3>
   <p>
   
<blockquote>
Bloodhorde plans to have raids of various dungeons and zones from time to time.
The main rule for raids is <u>be prepared</u>.  All raids will be announced
at least 5 days in advance.  If you plan on being at the raid, be there on time
and come prepared.  For preparation, we suggest visiting (if possible) the
place prior to the raid to know its location and layout.  <u>We strongly
suggest that you
print out a map of the raid zone so you do not get lost</u>. If we
say "everybody meet at the jail room on the 3rd floor", your answer should
be "ok", not "how do I get there?".

<p>Nothing wastes more time and risks more lives than an individual that
does not know the zone.  Getting lost leads to one thing, death, and death
can quickly turn a fun raid into a miserable experience.  If it is determined
that an individual has come to the raid ill-prepared, they will be asked to
leave.

<p>Also note that the gain of experience is the absolutely last concern on a
raid.  Raids often have 2 or more groups of people.  Therefore, it is unlikely
that any one group is going to get XP for every kill since all groups will most likely be fighting the same MoB.  Also, you may be grouped with somebody well beyond your level, which would cause you to get very little XP.  Raids are not for XP.  Raids are to go in and kill many things, obtain treasure, and have fun.  Whining about not getting experience during a raid is considered very bad form.  If you want experience, don't come to a raid.  However, if you want a chance at some good items and have fun, thats what a raid is about.
</blockquote>



